30.2  Undoing things in git

TL;DR
  - Committed work is almost never lost: the reflog remembers every place a
    branch has been. Uncommitted edits are the fragile part.
  - Pick the command by what you want back: a file, a commit, or a commit others
    already have.

      git restore --staged FILE     # unstage a file, keep the edits
      git restore FILE              # drop your edits to it: gone for good
      ⚠ changes or deletes data: check what it targets first
      git reset --soft HEAD~1       # undo the last commit, keep its changes
      git revert COMMIT             # undo a pushed commit with a new commit
      git reflog                    # where HEAD has been: find lost commits

Pitfalls
  - git reset --hard, git restore, git checkout -- FILE and git clean -f throw
    away uncommitted work: git has no copy of it.
  - Don't amend or reset commits others already pulled: use git revert, which
    undoes by adding a commit.
  - git stash before a risky command keeps your edits aside; git stash pop
    brings them back.
  - git clean -n lists what git clean -f would delete.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 30.2 --why

